From 285cee34b2371be5ca592640e81fd4f3fd568094 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:59:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: verisilicon: Add H.264 encoding to the Rockchip VEPU2
 of the RK3588

The VEPU2 encoder encodes H.264 Constrained Baseline, Main and High
profile I and P pictures, with CAVLC or CABAC, from YUV 4:2:0 and
YUYV/UYVY pictures up to 1920x1088. On the RK3588, it is the encoder half
of the VPU121 at 0xfdb50000, next to the VDPU2 decoder at offset 0x400,
which is the only part of it the driver uses so far. The four VEPU121
cores at 0xfdba0000 are JPEG encoders only: they ignore the H.264 mode
and produce a JPEG scan.

Add a V4L2 stateless H.264 encoder for it, on top of the stateless encode
core: the core prepares the SPS, PPS and per-picture encode parameters
from the controls, writes the parameter sets, runs the rate control and
manages the reference and reconstructed pictures, which are internal to
the driver.

The hardware writes the slice NAL unit itself, including the slice
header, which constrains the stream:
- pic_order_cnt_type is 2 and frame_num has 16 bits,
- every picture is a reference, with a single reference picture for P
  pictures and the sliding window marking process,
- a single slice, idr_pic_id has 4 bits.
The state_constrain operation enforces this, and the encode core reports
the parameters actually used through the controls. The nal_ref_idc of the
slice NAL unit is patched to the requested non-zero value once encoded.

The parameter sets are written by the CPU at the start of the destination
buffer. The hardware appends to them from a 64-bit aligned address, the
last bytes of the headers being passed in the STR_HDR_REM registers and
the bit offset in STREAM_START_OFFSET.

The QP is constant over a picture: the hardware macroblock rate control
(checkpoints and MAD based QP adjustment) is not used, and the frame
level rate control of the core (direct QP, constant quality or constant
bitrate) uses the stream size read back from the hardware. The average QP
is read back for debugging.

All the encoder registers are programmed for each picture, the ones that
are not used are cleared, so that no state is left over from JPEG jobs
running on the same core.

Add an RK3588 VPU121 variant with the encoder and the decoder, matched
on "rockchip,rk3588-vpu121". Its encoder only exposes
V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE, JPEG being left to the VEPU121 cores. Device
trees that only describe the decoder interrupt keep working: the encoder
interrupt is optional, and the encoder is not exposed without it. The
coded size is set with the crop rectangle of the source, which must fall
in its last macroblocks.

The other VEPU2 users (RK3399, PX30, RK3568) are left alone, they were
not tested.

The register programming and the mode decision tables are ported from
rk3399_vpu_hw_h264e.c in the ChromeOS kernel (chromeos-4.4 branch,
drivers/media/platform/rockchip-vpu/, Copyright (C) 2016 Rockchip
Electronics, GPL-2.0), which was driven by a userspace library. The
control set follows the VC8000E driver of the stateless encode series
(https://patch.msgid.link/20260522101653.2565125-13-paulk@sys-base.io).
The meaning of the hardware slice header and of the stream offset
registers was checked against the Rockchip MPP userspace driver
(hal_h264e_vepu2_v2.c), no code was taken from it.

